ating.\n    By 2030, automated theorem proving is likely robust, but \"new argument\" implies discovery.\n    I'll set 2050 around 0.5 or slightly lower because these problems are exceptionally hard. Maybe 0.35 is more realistic for *one* of them?\n    Actually, Riemann, P vs NP, Yang-Mills... if one falls, does it help others? Maybe not directly.\n    Let's set 2050 at 0.4.\n    2040 at 0.2.\n    2035 at 0.08.\n    2030 at 0.03.\n    2026 at 0.01.\n\n    Let's check the constraint: \"Give one probability for at least one qualifying solution by each deadline\".\n    Okay.\n    Let's finalize numbers to be realistic but acknowledging the speculative nature of AGI timelines.\n    2026: 0.01\n    2030: 0.05\n    2035: 0.15\n    2040: 0.30\n    2050: 0.55\n\n    Is 0.55 by 2050 too high? Most mathematicians might say lower. But AI capabilities are the main driver here. If AGI arrives in 2030-2040 window, math is likely smashed. If AGI is 2045+, then 2050 is tight.\n    Let's tone it down slightly to be more grounded in mathematical difficulty.\n    2026: 0.01\n    2030: 0.04\n    2035: 0.10\n    2040: 0.25\n    2050: 0.50\n\n    Check: Non-decreasing? Yes. Between 0 and 1? Yes.\n    JSON format? Yes.\n    No markdown/explanation? Yes.\n\n    Wait, I need to ensure keys are strings \"2026\", etc.\n    Double check the forecast date context. Forecast date is 2026-02-24.\n    Deadline 2026 is within the same year (Dec 31). Less than a year away. So 0.01 is appropriate.\n    Deadline 2050 is 24 years away.\n    The probabilities seem reasonable given the uncertainty and difficulty.\n\n    Final check on constraints: \"Return only one JSON object... with no markdown or explanation.\"\n\n    Let's adjust 2050 to 0.60 to reflect the high likelihood of AI assistance being ubiquitous and solving hard problems eventually.\n    Actually, keeping it at 0.50 is safer given the \"Millennium\" status implies extreme difficulty.\n    Let's stick with 0.45 for 2050 to be cautious.\n    